> /sbin/init.d/xlogin start, works ... then exit for CDE and
> /sbin/ini.d/xlogin/stop but I found it isn't very clean.

Well, if you want to do it in a _very_ basic way, you must, at a
minimum, achieve two things:
  - start the X server
  - start (at least) one first client
You could start experimenting with a script like like this:

  #!/bin/sh

  DISPLAY=:0.0
  export DISPLAY
  Xdec &
  xpid=$!
  sleep 5
  dxterm
  kill $xpid

This is about the minimum you need to get a graphical session
running (and get out of it again). However:
 - Xdec needs root privileges, so you must either be root, or use
   some wrapper (like, sudo), or make Xdec SUID root (which may not
   be a good idea).
 - The script above may open up a gaping security hole unless you add
   some access control; this part is explained in
     man Xsecurity
 - In order for the session to be actually usable, you will also need
   to start some window manager: mwm comes with Tru64 (I think).
   fvwm2 (common on Linux) compiles smoothly on Tru64.
   You can start it manually from the dxterm for testing; if it works,
   you can start the window manager _instead_ of the dxterm as your
   first client.

Regards,

Timo Felbinger


