> >
> > and boot stop at single-use mode. Does this mean the hard drive is
> > bad.
> 
> Yep.  The "start unit failed" message means that the operating system tried
> to tell the drive to spin up, but it failed to do so for some [unstated]
> reason.

It might be that the disk has the infamous 'stiction' problem. Some hard
disks, like certain older IBM models (DHFS), some Quantums and others,
have the problem when shut down after a long time running, that the
heads stick to the disk surface so tightly that the motor has not enough
torque to loosen them. It often helps if you remove the disk from the
case, keep it in the hand and once you feel or hear that it tries to
spin up, rotate it quickly left and right (like the motor would do with
the platters). In some cases, one could even 'clap' the disk gently
against a harder barrier. Search the web for 'stiction' and hard disks,
and you will very likely see more detailed (and better) descriptions of
how to do it. 

Most disks also have a jumper that controls spin-up, either by a SCSI
command or immediatly after power is supplied.

