We're running a UNIX cluster based on Compaq tru64 5.1a.

A process is freezing at a fork() command, but the spawned child
process executes OK.
The parent process calls fork(), the spawned child then executes,
eventually being blocked by a semaphore, but the Parent process never
kicks back in. It doesn't seem to get beyond the fork.

The problem is intermittent, with no obvious pattern. 
The system administrators can't find anything wrong with the system
resources - apparently, there's plenty of spare room, and no problems
are being logged.
We're very confident that its not a semaphore deadlock problem.
The Parent process does have one file open when it forks, but the
child closes its reference to this, as its first operation.

This behaviour did occur under the previous version of UNIX, but very,
very infrequently (many weeks between), now it's happening all to
frequently (multiple times a day).

Any suggestions are welcome - I'm running out of ideas!

Thanks,
Jonathan


