> > > > A bunch of workstations (or any other box) with a distributed
> scheduler
> > > >
> > > > HA clusters/ Service Guard
> > > > A group of systems that watch each others backs, and lend a helping
> > > > hand if one system gets into trouble.
> > > >
> > > > These are all called clusters.
>
> ...
>
> > The third option can also be created with very little kernel intervention.
> > HP's service only has one Kernel service to make it work, a safety time
> > to handle unhangs.
>
> Hmmm.  I find the moniker 'high-availability cluster' somewhat difficult to
> justify for a system that leaves it to applications to handle storage
> redundancy (which your description above suggests it might).  Once the
> system takes responsibility for storage redundancy, considerably more kernel
> support is required.
>
> - bill

As I'm sure you know, most peoples HA clusters are primarily designed to
support applications that are not cluster aware. So an instance of an
application runs on just one member of the cluster at a time. In the event of
a failure the application is restarted on an alternative node on the cluster.

None of this requires any additional support from the kernel beyond normal
volume management, and supporting multiple IP addresses.

Now this is obviously not the way that a VMS cluster does it, but for most
situations it provides the needed level of HA.

The one case which is hard to deal with in these designs is when a kernel
temporarily hangs, the userland heartbeating used to show cluster member
health stops and if the hang is long enough the other members of the cluster
vote you dead and take over control of your disks.

If the system then un-hangs and, say continues with an IO operation to
disks that it no longer owns it would cause corruption. So a kernel timeout
is registered that causes a TOC if it's ever allowed to expire. Since this
runs at a higher SPL than any IO code it can make sure that once the rest
of the cluster has decided your dead, that you will die without causing
disk corruption.

As far as I'm aware the only concurrent access system currently running
on HP-UX is Oracle Parallel Server.

I look forward to finding out how these situations are dealt with in VMS/Tru64
clusters.


Cheers

Ken




