"Ken Green" <Ken.Green@kgcc.co.uk> wrote in message
news:3CE55859.61FF842B@kgcc.co.uk...

...

> I'm slightly confused by a comment
> you, and Jan C. Vorbr�ggen made implying that the block level caching
> was controlled by XFC, or will be when it actually works properly.

It's hard for me to comment in detail about XFC:  I know how *I'd* design
it, but have no real information about how Ian and friends actually did.

My guess, given how the VMS lock manager works, is that XFC will only extend
the current mechanisms rather than replace them (i.e., that block-level
caching will still be coordinated via the DLM, but that XFC will, perhaps
also using the DLM or its own messages, allow data to be sent directly from
one host's cache to another's, similar to the Oracle cache-fusion approach).

An interesting side-question is how all the above will interact with
RMS-level caching in VMS (I don't even know how the current VIOC mechanisms
interact with RMS-level caching, whether application-local or in RMS global
buffers:  there may be significant redundancy in memory use there, or RMS
may be able to tell the lower levels not to cache - but this would seem to
subvert some of the new XFC abilities).  You can think of both XFC and VIOC
as at least somewhat similar in principle to Unix's system cache (and I
think they interact with other use of system memory somewhat like Unix's
unified buffer cache approaches do), but RMS caching takes place on the
process rather than the system level (though is still managed across nodes
by DLM mechanisms, and is IIRC protected from direct access by errant
application code).

- bill





