> >
> > As I'm sure you know, most peoples HA clusters are primarily designed to
> > support applications that are not cluster aware.
>
> My first impression was that you were talking about multiple cooperating
> application instances, so that may have colored my response.
>
>  So an instance of an
> > application runs on just one member of the cluster at a time. In the event
> of
> > a failure the application is restarted on an alternative node on the
> cluster.
> >
> > None of this requires any additional support from the kernel beyond normal
> > volume management, and supporting multiple IP addresses.
>
> So how does this 'normal volume management' take over the storage that the
> application was using on the other node without any kernel support?  I
> suspect this requires at least a cascading fail-over plan for inheritance of
> storage resources (which I wouldn't quite call 'normal volume management').
> But if concurrent access by multiple application instances on different
> nodes is *not* part of what you're describing, it indeed significantly
> reduces the required level of kernel support.
>
> - bill

Your right, not having to do concurrent access makes life easy.

For soft failure the failover can be performed by umounting the
disks and deactivating the volume group on the current node,
and then activating it on another.

Node failures are of course even easier, the adoptive node just
activates the volume group and mount the filesystems.

For MC/ServiceGuard, a userland cmlvmd provides volume group
locking, but since the API to LVM is not published, the kernel doesn't
need to get involved. The LVM commands can check with the daemon.

Cheers

Ken




