Ken Green <Ken.Green@kgcc.co.uk> writes:

> The problem with coherence isn't affected by whether the disks are
> local to one system or to all, the problem is that if multiple
> copies exist in the memory of the members of the cluster how do you
> ensure if one member modifies all the other members invalidate their
> copies atomically.

Step one, take the unix buffer cache out and destroy that part of the
brain... VMS uses direct to the process IO, not IO to the kernel with
a copy to the process, so there can well be NO cache at all. RMS
Global buffers are the common exception here.
 
...

> It's a question of how the DLM keeps track, and when nodes
> effectively need to check with the DLM.

Second point, the DLM does not *enforce* anything. It is a totally
co-operative system. For any 'thing', devise, file, record, range of
byte addresses, queue, directory, user do-hickey, there is a `resource
name' that can be used to mediate access to the resource. (note, you
do not even need anything real for this to be usefull. So you can
create a resource and lock it on a node.  If that node fails, the
other nodes can now obtain the lock and take over from the failed
node, as an example.) 

> > > If you do it on a per file basis, it's much easier. At open time
> > > you check whether you are the only node accessing the file, or
> > > that all nodes accessing are only open for reading, in this case
> > > there is no problem with caching at both ends. However once a
> > > node opens a shared file for writes then the caching needs to be
> > > done in just one place, or as above checking for conflicts needs
> > > to be performed on a per IO basis, or write only basis with a
> > > local cache directory.

> > Yes, but it is in reality worse than you describe, as there are
> > all sorts of 'details' in extending, truncating etc that must be
> > totally right always.
> >

> In the DUX model, once concurrent access, with at least one writer,
> starts (it used the idea of opened with write enabled) then caching
> was only performed on the system that physically had the disk. In
> the shared disk access model of the HSC... etc, of XPs with FC, or
> multi initiator SCSI... what ever then I guess it's upto the DLM to
> decide where best to cache, the server local, client remote model of
> DUX ( or I guess LAVC, is that still around?)  then I guess it's
> easier to decide on who caches.
 
> Anyway, who's caching doesn't much matter. The point was only one
> system cached, that way there can be no coherency trouble, but
> clients see long access latencies.

That is shared access in my book. VMS does not do this at all. At the
most, a system will serve blocks for another systems class driver.
 
> Truncating doesn't affect this, it can be considered as just like
> any other write.  On Unix, files can be sparse, I seem to remember
> that VMS' view of files is somewhat different, so I have no idea
> whether it supports sparse files, if not Then I guess truncates are
> a different problem.

ODS files are extent based. Any file is a ordered list of extents of
blocks. (A block btw, is *defined* to be 512 bytes. It is up to the
driver to do the various fudging to make this so.) So the file system
is VERY different to any of the unix systems. It does not have to
provide anything other than block access to the process.  Although
truncation is simple in principle, doing it so it is safe and
completable is a far trickier matter! It is also why you get a big
performance hit cf unix file systms for some things.

The nice thing about VMS, is that the tools are good enough for you
to pick up a lot of the stuff on a running system. With the VMS FAQ
in one hand, and SDA in the other you are set. To begin anyway :)

Getting a copy of the ODS-2 specs from the SIG tapes from years
ago is a very good start. Once you have a handle on the file system,
then you can move out from there.

