Sorry, Paul, but the lack of a Unix-style buffer cache is more a detriment
than an asset to VMS.  VIOC is a step toward a VMS system-level cache, and
XFC another bigger one, but for most typical use the Unix approach is likely
better still - and really doesn't introduce any more sharing problems than
current VMS distributed caching/buffering mechanisms.

 VMS uses direct to the process IO, not IO to the kernel with
> a copy to the process, so there can well be NO cache at all.

Depends on how you look at it.  Only application-specified block I/O (or
'locate-mode' access, if that still exists) avoids a buffering layer - just
because it happens to be in P1 space rather than Sx space doesn't really
make much difference (except that other local processes can't share it).

 RMS
> Global buffers are the common exception here.

Another not-so-great excuse for Unix-style system caching.  RMS *could* have
chosen to create a Unix-like system cache instead of implementing 'global
buffers' way back when, but that didn't fit as well with RMS's layering
model (and real-time advocates may have preferred to have somewhat tighter
control over system memory use, though it's not clear that equivalent
controls couldn't be obtained by establishing quotas and/or priorities on
system cache use).

