> > that VMS' view of files is somewhat different, so I have no idea
> > whether it supports sparse files, if not Then I guess truncates are
> > a different problem.

Considered commenting on this before, but got lazy.  I'm not familiar with
the details of Unix-style byte-range locks, so it's possible that a truncate
can just take out a write-style range lock on the section truncated and have
it 'just work' (though since it would have to wait for *every* conflicting
read or write lock to be released, it could take a while).  VMS locks are
slightly different in that they operate on names and hence don't accommodate
amorphous (and potentially overlapping) entities like byte (or truncate)
ranges easily.  I don't know exactly how VMS handles byte-range locks
themselves let alone truncate ranges, but I suspect it is indeed (as Paul
suggests) a bit messy.

>
> ODS files are extent based. Any file is a ordered list of extents of
> blocks. (A block btw, is *defined* to be 512 bytes. It is up to the
