> truncation is simple in principle, doing it so it is safe and
> completable is a far trickier matter! It is also why you get a big
> performance hit cf unix file systms for some things.

Not sure that the use of extents makes any real difference here:  after all,
several Unix file systems are extent-based (XFS, VxFS, I think JFS).

>
> The nice thing about VMS, is that the tools are good enough for you
> to pick up a lot of the stuff on a running system. With the VMS FAQ
> in one hand, and SDA in the other you are set. To begin anyway :)
>
> Getting a copy of the ODS-2 specs from the SIG tapes from years
> ago is a very good start. Once you have a handle on the file system,
> then you can move out from there.

As I said, I'm not sure that the actual on-disk structure makes too much
difference.  Inodes correspond at least moderately closely to index-file
entries, etc.  The use of careful-update sequences in ODS-2 in contrast to
many Unix file systems (which range from using prayer plus fsck to using
transaction logs to protect integrity) *is* a significant difference,
however, especially in how the clustered file system works.

