I was always amused that RMS lived in system space, and the XQP was in
user space...
 
> ...
 
> > > Truncating doesn't affect this, it can be considered as just
> > > like any other write.  On Unix, files can be sparse, I seem to
> > > remember that VMS' view of files is somewhat different, so I
> > > have no idea whether it supports sparse files, if not Then I
> > > guess truncates are a different problem.
 
> Considered commenting on this before, but got lazy.  I'm not
> familiar with the details of Unix-style byte-range locks, so it's
> possible that a truncate can just take out a write-style range lock
> on the section truncated and have it 'just work' (though since it
> would have to wait for *every* conflicting read or write lock to be
> released, it could take a while).  VMS locks are slightly different
> in that they operate on names and hence don't accommodate amorphous
> (and potentially overlapping) entities like byte (or truncate)
> ranges easily.  I don't know exactly how VMS handles byte-range
> locks themselves let alone truncate ranges, but I suspect it is
> indeed (as Paul suggests) a bit messy.

Remember, we are allowed to lose blocks to n-space on crash etc, but
we must be able to recover totally normally. Even if we have multiple
extents, and cross bit-map blocks, and volumes, and another node does
a BIG extend of another file and runs out of space and all the XQPs
have to dump their free space cache etc... Plus some interesting
corner cases in timing and the like.

> > ODS files are extent based. Any file is a ordered list of extents
> > of blocks. (A block btw, is *defined* to be 512 bytes. It is up to
> > the driver to do the various fudging to make this so.) So the file
> > system is VERY different to any of the unix systems. It does not
> > have to provide anything other than block access to the process.
> > Although truncation is simple in principle, doing it so it is safe
> > and completable is a far trickier matter! It is also why you get a
> > big performance hit cf unix file systms for some things.
 
> Not sure that the use of extents makes any real difference here:
> after all, several Unix file systems are extent-based (XFS, VxFS, I
> think JFS).

> > The nice thing about VMS, is that the tools are good enough for
> > you to pick up a lot of the stuff on a running system. With the
> > VMS FAQ in one hand, and SDA in the other you are set. To begin
> > anyway :)

> > Getting a copy of the ODS-2 specs from the SIG tapes from years
> > ago is a very good start. Once you have a handle on the file
> > system, then you can move out from there.
 
> As I said, I'm not sure that the actual on-disk structure makes too
> much difference.  Inodes correspond at least moderately closely to
> index-file entries, etc.  The use of careful-update sequences in
> ODS-2 in contrast to many Unix file systems (which range from using
> prayer plus fsck to using transaction logs to protect integrity)
> *is* a significant difference, however, especially in how the
> clustered file system works.

