...

> All `just works'. (Thank Bill for a good bit of that!
> He was one of the original RMS developers.)

But only on the 11, not in the clustered VMS environment.

>
> > Sorry, Paul, but the lack of a Unix-style buffer cache is more a
> > detriment than an asset to VMS.
>
> Yes, but until recently, on unix systems all disk IO was to the
> kernel, never to userland. (for a large enought value of recently, and
> a small value of all :) The key factor is the buffer *may* exist,
> rather than *must*. So you can not use it as a sync point, as it may
> not be there at all. or it may be there in SOME nodes, but not others.

I'm not sure that's a significant distinction (any more, now that Unixes can
perform direct I/O).  After all, the local system cache isn't a distributed
synchronization point in clustered Unix environments either, and even local
Unix environments may (I don't know, but they could) coordinate cached data
with directly-accessed data (just as VMS could - and again I don't know if
it does) using lock-manager mechanisms.

Using global buffers you can make local RMS access work similarly to Unix
cached access in this regard.  Using Unix direct I/O mechanisms you could
make local Unix access work similarly to RMS access in this regard.  The
*big* differences between the two systems are the default behaviors (sharing
via caching using RMS global buffers is a bit of a pain, and duplicating the
caching instead using lower-level VIOC or XFC caches is wasteful) and the
flexible use of system memory that Unix caching allows.

...

> One thing VMS has done, is that buffer update go via the disk, never
> memory to memory although the SCS ports could do it no problem. I was
> told that failure modes where too horrid to handle with out a stable
> copy in the middle, so onto and off the disk... This may have been
> improved.

I think XFC was doing something in that area (but not in 7.3).  It is hard
to get right in many of the cases (e.g., distributed write after write:
what happens when the original owner issues a Flush in the middle of
things?).  There is, however, no need for the new owner to go to disk for
the data after it has been written:  it can just be forwarded from the
original owner.

>
> > >  RMS Global buffers are the common exception here.
>
> > Another not-so-great excuse for Unix-style system caching.  RMS
> > *could* have chosen to create a Unix-like system cache instead of
> > implementing 'global buffers' way back when, but that didn't fit as
> > well with RMS's layering model (and real-time advocates may have
> > preferred to have somewhat tighter control over system memory use,
> > though it's not clear that equivalent controls couldn't be obtained
> > by establishing quotas and/or priorities on system cache use).
>
> I was always amused that RMS lived in system space, and the XQP was in
> user space...

