others it's less so, I guess like all things it's a question of using it right.

>
>  VMS uses direct to the process IO, not IO to the kernel with
> > a copy to the process, so there can well be NO cache at all.

This copy avoidance can be good. On most (HP) systems the copy from
kernel space to user space is performed by the CPU, only the V-Class of
HP's systems has a hardware memory mover.

Using Veritas' VxFS (HP's marketing people call this JFS) you can bypass
the buffer cache and perform direct IO. However to do this (and actually
to get it to work) you have to do your IOs in DEV_BSIZE (usually 1K) multiples
and also start the IO from a 4K page boundary. If you break these rules
you either get errors or O_SYNC IO depending on how you tried to get direct IO.

