Pam wrote:
> I have a AlphaWorkstation XP1000 running v5.1A. The root partition is
> 99% full. I've looked in /dev and /tmp and there is nothing untoward
> in either of these. I've tried a reboot to clear anything hidden in
> /tmp but / still shows 99% full.
> 
> Can anybody please advise on what to try next?

        du -x / | sort -n

Find the directory that has the highest disk usage, cd to that directory 
then

        du -sk * | sort -n

Repeat as needed until you've found the disk space hog.  Beware of removing 
anything that you find unless you're *real* sure you can remove it.

Also, a common problem is that when creating a tar file or a [v]dump file 
to a tape, people will mis-type the tape drive name:

        vdump 0f /dev/nrmtoh /

This will result in a file by the name of "/dev/nrmtoh", when the user 
really wanted to type:

        vdump 0f /dev/nrmt0h /

Note the "o" vs. "0".  To see if this is a problem, do this:

        find /dev -type f -ls

On my 5.1A system, I only found a few files, not terribly large is size.

Then again, are you sure that the root partition was sufficiently large to 
begin with?

        Stu


