A recent interchange in comp.arch about the likelihood that EV8 actually
would have appeared on (its last) schedule in 2004 sent me on a trip down
memory lane in The Register archives - specifically, what a search on 'ev8'
turned up.

It turns out that I was correct in my contention that EV8 had already taken
its expectable overall schedule slip (hence the assertion that it would not
appear on time because no other Alpha generations ever did was
inapplicable - since it had already slipped by at least the normal amount).
In early 1999 The Register reported Compaq's expectation (even after the
decision to shove lock-step support into Alpha - though the consequences of
this may not yet have been reflected in schedules) that EV7 would appear in
Y2K and EV8 in 2001.  The 2001 EV8 date even appeared as late as a Y2K
Register article, and an early 2002 date for EV8 was mentioned late in Y2K.

Of course, this was in the context of a 1 GHz EV68 appearing in late 1999
(later, late Y2K).  Funny how *all* Alpha development got really, really
sluggish about 3 years ago, isn't it?  Samsung supposedly already had 1 GHz
Alphas running in 1999, but somehow they took two more years to get out into
the market.  And in the same time period EV7 slipped from early 2000 to late
2002 (reportedly at least largely due to cutbacks and demoralization), and
EV8 from 2001 to early 2002 to 2004.

Now, 1999 was when Curly and Robison were (as Robison stated in his recent
Register interview) starting to decide how best to terminate Alpha
development - at about the same moment that Bill Heil and Jesse Lipcon were
publishing Compaq's earnest expression of long-term commitment to the
platform.  Which makes the sudden lack of any Alpha development urgency (and
the cancellation of NT on Alpha, and consequently Win64 on Alpha) much more
understandable, but at the same time makes the lies about Alpha's inability
to retain its performance lead over Itanic (which seems to be between 2 and
3 years now and would be even greater if the slow-down hadn't occurred) that
much more blatant.

Another reminder the Register archives brought to light was Pfeiffer's
vision for Alpha - which the brouhaha during the DEC acquisition may have
obscured somewhat.  Pfeiffer was laying the groundwork for exactly the kind
of offensive that many in comp.os.vms have long been advocating:
positioning Alpha as an alternate 'industry-standard' platform for the
64-bit world in direct competition with Itanic (which was already beginning
to look vulnerable back then:  "Alpha is at least two years ahead of
Merced" - Pfeiffer, June, 1998).  NT and Win64 on Alpha were integral parts
of that offensive, as was Tru64 - and while one could debate whether
Pfeiffer truly appreciated VMS he certainly gave it far better exposure than
Palmer before him or Curly later and was thus likely open to being educated.
Pfeiffer's post-acquisition statements (as contrasted with pre-acquisition
comments) indeed reflected an increased appreciation for the platform he had
bought - which would have boded well for Alpha's future had he remained at
the helm (especially after Alpha had Win64 to itself for close to two years
before Merced even appeared - not that its appearance seems to have actually
mattered).

And Pfeiffer stood head and shoulders above bean-counters like Palmer and
Capellas.  He captained Compaq's turn-around starting in 1991 and its later
explosive growth, and seemed ready to do the same with the DEC assets.
Unfortunately, this strategy appears not to have been acceptable to Ben
Rosen and the other Compaq directors - which is the basis for the 'vision
vs. incompetence' in this topic's title.

There were rumors at the time (at least according to other, non-Register
articles that surfaced during my excursion today) that Rosen wasn't happy
with Pfeiffer's handling of Compaq.  It is true that Dell was overtaking
Compaq in the PC product space at that time, but subsequent events make it
clear that if Pfeiffer was concentrating at least as much on his
higher-margin server and enterprise business (as he appeared to be) this was
*exactly* the right course of action to have been taking.  And while the
Dell threat was real in the PC end of the business, it hardly seems to have
alarmed Compaq investors:  Compaq's stock hit an all-time high of $51.25 in
January, 1999 - when the Dell threat was already very visible.

Of course, Curly didn't succeed in meeting the Dell challenge either,
despite three years' additional time to work on it - and while throwing the
core of his enterprise business (except Tandem) into the toilet.  And during
that period (since January, 1999, during which the Dow vacillated and wound
up rising over 10% and the NASDAQ vacillated and wound up falling about 25%)
Compaq stock lost close to 80% of its value.

Now the Compaq BoD is history, but Curly's smiling, lying, incompetent
countenance still graces the heights of HP's hierarchy.  And according to
both him and Carly they're just two peas in a pod, so one shouldn't expect
significant change from the past three years at Compaq.

Enjoy.

- bill

P.S.  For more on Eckhard's plans for Alpha, try a Google search on "Eckhard
Pfeiffer" and search within the results for Alpha.  Or drop by
www.alphant.com/news.





