> prove/disprove. (DB is Oracle). Being a complete newbie to TRU64 any
> point in the right direction would be much appreciated.
> 
> Cheers

There are number of benchmarks that will run on TRU64. Most of them you 
will have to compile yourself, so make sure you have a compiler 
installed on your system. I/O benchmarks which will work for you are of 
two basic types: the ones which measure the performance of a filesystem 
and the ones that work on a raw device. You mention DBA,so I am assuming 
we are talking about a database performance. Depending on which database 
you have and whether it is configured to use raw disk/partition or a 
filesystem you might want to choose a different benchmark. Here are the 
ones I can remember off hand:
A. raw disk benchmarks
1. lmbench (particularly lmdd part of it)
	http://www.bitmover.com/lmbench/
	This is my favourite one, but it is somewhat convoluted and
	poorly documented.
2. SPC-1
	http://www.storageperformance.org/showpage.php?page=index.html
	I never tried this one

B. filesystem benchmarks
lots of those.
They measure different parameters too. Here is a link to a list of many 
of them: http://www.acnc.com/04_02.html
Look at Bonnie ++, iozone and postmark. postmark is for small 
files/transactions so it might be suitable for database-like 
comparisons. Bonnie is pretty universal one.

Want to mention also: do not compare your results with results obtained 
by someone else unless it is well-standardised, which they almost never 
are.
Take a well-working array and the array in question and compare them. If 
you do not have a good one to compare to, take a disk with known and 
proven specs (like fujitsu) and compare to it. This will give you an 
extra reference point. Array should obviously perform better then a 
single disk, but it will never be the sum of the disk count. A decent 
RAID-5 array with 8 drives should give you anywhere between 2xDISK to 
4xDISK performance. Obviously this will vary greatly depending on the 
array, benchmark, machine running benchmark, setup and a zillion other 
factors.

Good luck!
-- 
Dennis

