I've tried various methods to speed this up, but TCP has some rules
that it is actually never supposed to take down a socket, so there are
qute a few workarounds that are already in the code. I have found it
takes about a minute to get a socket back in my application.

Best option if you can is to nail up the socket and not open and close
it all the time. It seems to be either this or live with it. There are
some parameters in sysconfig for tcp and sockets, but I have found
them to be very ineffective at actually changing this behaviour. Some
of these are

tcp_keepalive_default = 1
tcp_keepinit = 30
tcp_keepidle = 30
tcp_keepintvl = 30
tcp_keepcnt = 2
socket
sominconn = 65535
somaxconn = 65535


I have also occasionally tried the REUSEPORT attribute, but that is a
good way to get the system to hang (quite hard as I have found out)


