The main user was CAD systems, if you sell workstations these guys are
your bread and butter.

At the time 10Mbits was a reasonable speed for disk. The HP systems at the
time used HP-IB (IEEE488) with a tweaked handshake. This could only
deliver 1MB/s so a 10Mb lan was as fast as the disk interface. The typical
workstation disk (say a HP7957 or 58) probably only deliver about 350KB/s
The server disks (HP7936/37) would do the whole 1MB/s, I'm sure when I
compared these to the RA81s on VAX I used to run, the HP disks were quicker.
So the LAN link speed was not an issue.

These cluster were designed to be cheap, CI was not a cheap solution.
Using operational systems as the disk controllers was cheaper than
having dedicated disk controllers like the CI bus clusters. (Sorry I can't
remember the name of the box that connected the shared disks to the
cluster)

As I said there is more than one definition of a cluster and different solutions
to different to different problems.

LAVC, was running DEC clusters over 10Mb LANs, and could get no where
need link speed on the uVax II s we had at Rapid.

>
>         Certainly not a business performing a lot of transactions even
>         15-20 years ago.
