"Ken Green" <Ken.Green@kgcc.co.uk> wrote in message
news:3CE216FD.65DA2AB1@kgcc.co.uk...

...

> For HP-UX  there has been at least three totally different non overlapping
> uses of the word cluster.
>
> Diskless clusters (although that doesn't mean systems couldn't have disks)
> This made a group of workstations act like a large single system, it was
> nothing like NFS diskless. All the systems had a single common root
directory
> and filesystem. 99.9% of admin was just the same as it would have been for
> a single stand alone system.
> I suspect if you asked most workstation or PC users what they would like
> a network to look like, it would be like this.
>
> Computer clusters/snake farms.
> A bunch of workstations (or any other box) with a distributed scheduler
>
> HA clusters/ Service Guard
> A group of systems that watch each others backs, and lend a helping
> hand if one system gets into trouble.
>
> These are all called clusters.

I suspect that the point you are missing is that VMS clustering (from the
very beginning) completely covered the first and third kinds of 'different'
clusters you describe above, and to at least some degree the second as well
(though the distributed scheduler would have to have been an add-on).  Since
the second may be the easiest to create outside the base OS (see Beowulf), I
don't consider that a significant lack.

It really is the case that no one has significantly extended the VMS
clustering mechanisms since they were created 18 years ago, and that most
have only implemented subsets of them.  VMS even provides the basic
mechanisms (via the lock manager) for multiple distributed instances of a
single application to coordinate its own clustered cooperation and
operational redundancy, though this is an area where other systems *may* (I
just don't happen to know) have come up with more application hand-holding
facilities than VMS supports.  And AFAIK *no one* except IBM's Parallel
Sysplex has a fully-distributed clustered file system equivalent to VMS's:
other file systems (both vendor and third-party) depend upon fail-over
(rather than concurrent) shared storage access for at least metadata and
usually for data as well, with the resulting melange of cascading fail-over
scripts to determine who gets what portion of the file system after multiple
failures occur (plus the potential for hot spots due to the served nature of
the metadata and, usually, data).  Plus, VMS also supports use of
host-private storage exported as shared (with full distributed redundancy
mechanisms) for situations where that may be preferable to use of true
concurrently-shared storage.

- bill





