I think you'll appreciate it.

First, a distributed cache (rather than a server-local cache that clients
take advantage of) is *always* preferable as long as the (very low latency)
benefit you obtain from being able to cache your own hot data locally (and
access it without any distributed interactions) exceeds the overhead of
having to coordinate access to data across the cluster (which only really
applies to data that at least someone is updating frequently and others need
frequently).  Note that even in many interchange-intensive applications,
while a 'served' copy of the data *may* be somewhat more efficient than the
distributed cache approach, it may well be even better to centralize the
operation entirely (i.e., ship the access *functions* to a central point
rather than keep bouncing the data back and forth between the server and
those who need it).

So even with the slow links of a decade ago distributed caching could make
sense for many common access patterns.

Second, even when the data you need is not cached locally, it costs very
little more to obtain it from some other node that *does* have it cached
than from a central server:  a 3-cornered message (requestor to coordinator
to actual cacher, and response to requestor) rather than a 2-message
request/response.  This is I believe what Oracle now does and what XFC will
eventually do.  Since with optimized protocol stacks one-way message latency
can be reduced to under 100 us. (and under 10 us. with hardware-enhanced
mechanisms such as VI), and since sending even just a 4 KB 'block' across a
100 Mbit link takes about 400 us., the difference in that environment at
worst nearly negligible (and with something like VI nearly negligible even
using a gigabit link, though in that case the additional interrupt required
in the 3-corned case may start to become noticeable).

