If you need to get to the data on a disk, the I/O rate will be the same
whether a bunch of hosts access it directly or the same bunch of hosts
access it through a server (modulo the inefficiences introduced by served
access).  The only exceptions being isochronous access where the server can
decide whom to screw if the total rate exceeds the disk's capacity to serve
it.

Similar observations apply to sharing connection bandwidth:  the disk can
supply only as much bandwidth as whatever pipe it's connected to can manage,
regardless, so while a switched back-end storage network used for shared
disks must be able to distribute that bandwidth to whatever combination of
hosts require it this doesn't differ much from the need for the front-end
network that served storage uses to distribute the load (again, unless
isochronous access is at issue).

