> What we have is a multi threaded server process with n working threads,
> which should spread the workload over n worker single threaded processes.
> Each work thread should store the comm channel (msgq, socket, pipe ...) in
> his own thread specific data. So each thread always contacts the same
worker
> process.

The way I've seen people using threads is:

- there is one (or more) process that is the SERVER
- server has multiple threads, one of them is LISTENER, others are WORKERS
- listener accepts requests and dispatches it to one of the workers
- there can be an additional thread, POOLER, which monitors the number of
free workers and creates/destroys them if need be

In this implemenatation, workers do not have comm channel information (and
really, they shouldn't). Each time there is a request, listener will take
it, re-package it (if need be) and hand it over to a worker thread. If you
opt for sockets, listener will pass the socket pointer to a worker and
continue to listen on the socket descriptor for the next request.

Nix.






