"Paul Repacholi" <prep@prep.synonet.com> wrote in message
news:87offgrprl.fsf@k9.prep.synonet.com...

...

> Although VMS clusters are described as a shared storage
> system, really they are a shared nothing system. They do not share,
> they co-ordinate. So, all the drives on a HSC or HSJ on the CI are
> *local* disks for ALL the hosts.

I'm afraid the description above is what most of the industry (IME) calls
'shared storage', since each host has direct physical access to the storage
(analogous to the shared memory in an SMP) and shares it essentially
concurrently.  'Shared-nothing' usually refers to a system where each
storage unit (and each unit of everything else, like memory) is private to
some host and accessible only through that host (even though that host may
export it for shared use as VMS exports MSCP devices - which is sometimes
called pseudo-shared storage).

