> > If you try to do it on a per IO basis, then every read or write
> > needs to check, so you loose the advantage of local caching. You
> > could minimise this to only impacting writes I guess but that would
> > require all systems to maintain a cache directory for the whole
> > cluster.
>
> No, only if you no longer 'own' the required access to the
> resource. So a burst of IO will often complete with only a single trip
> to the disk.
>

It's a question of how the DLM keeps track, and when nodes effectively
need to check with the DLM.

>
> > If you do it on a per file basis, it's much easier. At open time you
> > check whether you are the only node accessing the file, or that all
> > nodes accessing are only open for reading, in this case there is no
> > problem with caching at both ends. However once a node opens a
> > shared file for writes then the caching needs to be done in just one
> > place, or as above checking for conflicts needs to be performed on a
> > per IO basis, or write only basis with a local cache directory.
>
> Yes, but it is in reality worse than you describe, as there are all
> sorts of 'details' in extending, truncating etc that must be totally
> right always.
>

In the DUX model, once concurrent access, with at least one writer, starts
(it used the idea of opened with write enabled) then caching was only
performed
on the system that physically had the disk. In the shared disk access model

of the HSC... etc, of XPs with FC, or multi initiator SCSI... what ever
then I guess it's upto the DLM to decide where best to cache, the server
local, client remote model of DUX  ( or I guess LAVC, is that still
around?)
then I guess it's easier to decide on who caches.

Anyway, who's caching doesn't much matter. The point was only one system
cached, that way there can be no coherency trouble, but clients see long
access
latencies.

Truncating doesn't affect this, it can be considered as just like any other
write.
On Unix, files can be sparse, I seem to remember that VMS' view of files is

somewhat different, so I have no idea whether it supports sparse files, if
not
Then I guess truncates are a different problem.

>
> > Sorry I don't know how VMS tackles these issues, it has been more
> > years than I care to remember since I used a VMS cluster, and back
> > then had no idea how an OS worked. I'm sure VMS clusters have moved
> > on a long way since then anyway.
>
> They are very much as they where then, but with LOTS of detail
> changes. IE, there is no longer a master directory. Now all the nodes
> build their own copy of the 'directory vector' so they can determine
> locally which node is the default master of a resource. As each node
> executes the identical code, all the vectors are identical. If not,
> the Fat Owl will dump on your console ;)
