uschmidt@mpi-halle.de (Udo Schmidt) wrote in message news:<cddc549b.0205162250.33cba73@posting.google.com>...
> Hi, we have to enlarge our ES45 system from 1 GB to 28GB RAM because
> of some special new jobs bigger than 8 GB. Normally we had swap space
> twice of
> memory, but now ? And what's about core dumps ? I like to partitioning
> a new hard disk. Has anyone experiences suggestions how to handle in
> such a constellation ?

first of all 28Gb RAM and/or 60 gb os Swap space is not a
constellation. GS320 goes up to 1/2 Tb of RAM, more than one digit
more.

Having a huge swap space depends on your application behavior. If the
peak need on VM is under real memory, you will not swap / do paging at
all. In that case, keep just a little slice of swap space to handle
partial crash dumps (2 Gb is enough)and configure swap mode to
deferred. Actually, if you don't care about crash dumps, you just can
have no swap at all in deferred mode.

If you are actually paging out, swap space must be large enough to
store all pages out of memory. If you fill the swap space you'll get
into trouble. This space can be low or very large, even several times
your RAM size. In eager mode, there is simply no relationship between
RAM size and swap space. Swap space needed is "only" peak VM usage in
your system life (sum of all VM address spaces of all processes at a
certain time).

My best advice to know this value : Use eager mode for swap with a
huge swap space defined. Check the usage (not reservation) by swapon
-s. Then go to deferred mode (reboot needed) and set your swap space
to 1.5 times the peak usage.

Good luck,


