>
> It is, Oracle Parallel Server for example uses this mechanism with
> a DLM coordinating the access to the shared storage.
>
> Failover clusters do the same thing, with the failover manager
> coordinating access to the shared storage.

No.  Truly shared storage allows concurrent access by multiple hosts.
Fail-over-style shared storage serves the storage from one host to the
others:  while at least one more host is *connected* to the storage so that
it can take over coordination for it if the current coordinator fails, it
does not access the storage concurrently with the host currently responsible
for it.

Intermediate environments to exist where there is a fail-over coordinator
for metadata but all the hosts can to at least some extent access user data
on the storage directly and concurrently.  I believe that Tru64 V5.1 offers
such facilities, as well as several third-party products (well, IBM and HP
bought a couple of them so perhaps they're not exactly 'third-party' any
more).

- bill





