In article <c2TC8.134772$v7.11846334@bin6.nnrp.aus1.giganews.com>,
Bill Todd <billtodd@metrocast.net> wrote:
> Why?

Well, apparently I was under the false impression that producing the best
possible merged UNIX was somewhere sensible on the priority list.

> It would certainly seem to result in a better product, but if the cost
> is *any* disruption of the existing HP-UX base it's *really* hard to imagine
> they'd do that (leaving aside big-endian/little-endian issues) - especially
> since they've already made it clear that the HP-UX market is the one they
> intend to concentrate on.

The BSD and Mach codebases are pretty clean, and run both ways, so I
don't think endianism would be that big a deal, in the kernel at
least.

The thing is, UNIX has a pretty hard line between kernel and userspace.
There's a relatively small API, and it's tightly specified. You don't
have any random subsystem designer adding entries to a call gate and
all of a sudden you have 60 new calls in the API.

And I'm familiar with the code, or at least its cousin.

I did a bunch of tweaking on my FreeBSD 1.1 system to get other-BSD
code to work on it, back when BSDI was going 4.4 and they had the only
386 BSD Netscape. The kernel-side of the 4.4-emulation under 4.3 was
amazingly small... mostly mapping 64-bit args to 32-bit. 4.2 under 4.3
shouldn't be significantly harder, especially if you don't need to
maintain both APIs.

Though you could.

All of which is apparently academic now.

