> > many of the people who design these systems), at DEFCON.

> > And how many of the ones who *are* there, know anything about VMS?

> they were given a crash course before the event ... vms is not hard
> to understand, esp. to those at defcon

You HAVE to be pulling my leg.

VMS is an enormously complex system, with a huge array of interrelated sets
of privileges. I'm sure that most of the obvious privilege-elevation attacks
in the old VMS have been resolved, but that's still a whole class of attacks
that's completely unlike UNIX... and while NT has a similar privilege model,
you don't normally need to bother with it because NT boxes are not normally
set up with any significant internal security... the trick with NT isn't to
get 'root', it's to get in.

> ... and now they have had a
> whole year to prepare for defcon10 ... and we will see again that
> you cannot root a properly configured vms system!

If the system is properly configured, it doesn't matter what OS it's running:
if it's not listening on any sockets you can't get into it.

The trick is getting into the system once it's been opened up and you can
start to look at the possibility of executing code on it. What webserver
are you running on the VMS system, for example, and what kind of application
hosting does it provide?

AND in any case, you keep missing the hints I keep pitching you about the
relationship between hackers and DEFCON.

