> The Montreal Compaq office has said a few times that Tru64's current
> clustering capabilities now include ALL the functionality that was present
 in
> openVMS (past tense is theirs, not mine).
>
> I do not know if that is really true or not. If it is true, it means that
 any
> vendor can develop clustering technology that rivals VMS in a very short
time.

'Functionality' is a slippery concept.  I've argued that Sun clusters
approach the functionality of VMS clusters, and that's true, but they suffer
markedly in file system scalability and performance, because their file
system is simply NFS (enhanced to support transparent, if
less-than-immediate, server fail-over) rather than direct-to-disk with
distributed cache.

Tru64's file system is similar:  it's exported (like NFS or RMS/DAP) rather
than direct-to-disk, but it *does* apparently have a distributed cache
(presumably coordinated using the DLM it got from VMS), which should give it
performance intermediate between Sun and VMS clusters in typical situations.
Can't scale as far or as flexibly as VMS, though, since it (like Sun) is
limited to the load-capacity of a single server to export its private -
though fail-overable - portion of the file system (and the file system must
be re-partitioned if the load on an existing data partition exceeds the
capacity of a single server node).

My guess would be that there are other performance/availability/whatever
differences too.  Whether any of them constitute 'functional' differences is
in the eye of the beholder.

- bill

