	is constantly being re-invented re-defined and Pfister will be out
	with a 3rd edition someday to prove it.

	Along these lines... it is readily apparent why many are using
	"failover" clustering.  There is a great inherent advantage.  All
	locks are local, all storage access is local.  So very large SMP
	nodes stay popular, Regatta, UE15000, etc. with a twin idle box
	sitting there, "just in case."

	Understanding issues (1) and in response was/is Galaxy where clustered
	nodes share a backplane, node communication goes through shared memory,
	shared disk, shared memory, and for a next feature (be a while)
	will be to move the lock trees (DLM) into shared memory so that
	message passing SCS doesn't occur for locking.  Until then, "failover"
	clustering of 32 CPUs (with an underlying well written database) 
	should have an advantage over 4 - 8 CPU Galaxy nodes in a Marvel.  
	However, I think Galaxy in Marvel even with "remote" locks, should do 
	real well at performance scaling anyhow.

