> OK I'll bite,
>
> so how do you get a cohertent buffer cache with multiple systems accessing
> the same disk without using some sort of network?

So how do multiple systems access any shared data at all (whether on shared
disk or not) without using some sort of network?

With non-shared disks, if you want to share data among systems they need to
cooperate, just as they need to using shared disks.  The primary difference
due to the sharing of the disk is that when desired data isn't cached
somewhere the system that wants it can, after obtaining any necessary locks,
get it directly from (or send it directly to) the disk rather than ask some
other system to get it and send it on.

In large systems, this means that  1) there's no need to partition the data
among the various systems that 'serve' it (because all systems have direct
access to all data),  2) there's no problem after partitioning said data
with a server getting overloaded due to hot spots (because, again, all data
is directly accessible to all who need it), and  3) the storage can be
scaled independently of the processing capacity (and without the need to
repartition).

And eliminating the need for 'server'-style operation does not increase the
network traffic (thus does not increase access latency).  Obtaining a lock
from whoever is in a position to grant it (which is usually known by the
requestor - but I'm not going to reprise the VMS distributed lock manager
algorithms here) takes one exchange, after which the requestor can obtain
the data from disk, which is the same number of operations that would occur
if instead the requestor asked a 'server' to obtain the data and forward it.
(Actually, it can be less, because the VMS DLM algorithms make it likely
that most lock requests are local, hence the disk access can take place
directly.)

With a good distributed cache (such as the one Oracle recently developed for
its 'cache fusion' architecture) you can do even better due to the ability
to combine cache-management messages with lock-management messages (and once
you have such a distributed cache, shared storage becomes a definite win
over served storage).  VMS unfortunately dropped the ball on this for many
years, but is finally getting there with XFC (when they get it working
right).  Meanwhile, there is the legitimate knock that a node that wants
access to data that another node has cached must still go to disk for it
(whereas if it was cached at a 'server' then no disk access would be
required) - but this is an anomaly of the VMS implementation rather than an
inherent problem with concurrent disk-sharing.

- bill





