Rob Young wrote:

> >
> > BTW HP-UX had shared disk cluster from HP-UX 6.0 through to 9
> > before dropping them.
> >
>
>         Couldn't get it right, it seems.
>
>

Not at all, they got it right first time, but times change.

The Diskless Ux environment gave full SVID compliant system behaviour
for a smallish group of workstaions. It included full coherency for the
buffer caches, in most case this worked fine and quickly, but we tended
to find some programs (mostly Fortran but some C) which tended to open
files read/write unnessessarily, and so force the double caching to be dropped
in favour of disk owner caching.

However in order to make this work at a reasonable speed when it first
came out the decision was made to use raw IEEE 802.3 LAN packets
rather than a nice high level protocol like TCP/IP or DECNET, since most
systems LAN performance was CPU power limited rather than wire
speed limited.

At the time this came out I was working for DECs largest UK distributor,
and will never forget the face on one of the salesman faces when half way
through a demo to some customers of LAVC he pointed to the LAN traffic
monitor behind him (without looking) and said something along the lines of
"And as you can see, with all these VAXs in the building clustered we rarely
see the network above 4% utilisation, so you can see Ethernet has plenty of
bandwidth", to which the customer said "Hmmmm...... well it look pretty
well saturated to me", no one had warned me, and I was was doing some
stress testing forcing a workstation to page over the network.

The downside to this approach was that it was a single LAN based design.
It couldn't be routed, all the clients needed to be on the same network
segment, it didn't even like a lot of bridges, since at the time a lot of these
couldn't really keep up with a full 10Mb link.

But eventually it was killed because it wasn't standard, one big customer
(The US Navy I think) wanted NFS diskless clusters for security (not sure
how you can ever equate NFS with security) and so DUX was killed. Only
to be replaced with NFS based diskless. However as soon as people realised
that this wasn't like a DUX cluster they bought disks for all there machines.
(Including the customer who had made sure DUX was knifed)

Diskless clusters were invented to tackle the problem that disk were
expensive. It so happens the solution to this problem also solved the
a part of the Admin is expensive problem.

When the disks are expensive problem went away, and someone killed
the easy admin solution. Diskless clusters went away too.

As I say... times change

Cheers

Ken



