The problem comes when someone else is caching the data. How do you
cope. Do you manage cache coherency on a per IO basis or on a per file
basis.

If you try to do it on a per IO basis, then every read or write needs to check,

so you loose the advantage of local caching. You could minimise this to only
impacting writes I guess but that would require all systems to maintain a
cache directory for the whole cluster.

If you do it on a per file basis, it's much easier. At open time you check
whether you are the only node accessing the file, or that all nodes accessing
are only open for reading, in this case there is no problem with caching at
both ends. However once a node opens a shared file for writes then the
caching needs to be done in just one place, or as above checking for
conflicts needs to be performed on a per IO basis, or write only basis
with a local cache directory.

Sorry I don't know how VMS tackles these issues, it has been more years
than I care to remember since I used a VMS cluster, and back then had
no idea how an OS worked. I'm sure VMS clusters have moved on a
