There are no binary configuration files in System V derived
systems. Solaris is a good example of this - it doesn't have
a GUI configuration utility like SAM (admintool can be safely
discounted). Everything that can and needs to be configured
is done through editable files in /etc:

/etc/system: OS and kernel modules parameters
/etc/inittab: configuration of the mother of all processes
/etc/inetd.conf: networking services
/etc/config.d and /etc/rc?.d: start/stop scripts
/etc/vfstab: mountable devices
/etc/auto*: automounter configuration
etc. 

There are some networking configuration tools that
act directly on the kernel (ifconfig, route, plumb etc),
but they are driven through editable configuration files

Once you are aware of the main configuration files,
getting any Unix system up and running is relatively
easy. It also helps one's understanding of what the
GUI configurators do. 

>    And nothing in the typical Linux distribution points out which tools
>    are in common with other vendor's Linux, so the kid has no way to
>    know which tools to choose.

Linux is enough like Unix that a book on Unix system
administration will be an excellent foundation for any
motivated person wanting to understand how Linux is
configured.

Take SuSE's YaST, for example, which is a tool a bit like 
SMIT or SAM. It's driven by a configuration file that you
can read to understand what commands YaST (the studlycaps
are theirs) invokes, and what principal configuration files
it modifies. The knowledge about primary configuration files
(e.g. "man inittab") is usable on any version of Unix.
 
>    How minor the differences are depend on what work you're doing.  At
>    the end user level all UNIX are pretty much the same. 

To the end user, the OS doesn't matter. I've seen people who
don't see the difference between Word and StarOffice Writer
("Hey, that's a cute version of Word you're using").

>    At the programmer level real differences start to show up. 

I write portable programs for a living, and I can assure
you that 95% of the code is identical between all the
platforms - if you write code with some attention to the
published standards (such as POSIX), it's even higher. 

>    To the admin every UNIX is dramaticaly different. 

Not in my experience. Granted, they are different, and SMIT
and SAM look different - but frankly, there are as many, if not
more, differences between the various incarnations of Windows
(from the same vendor) as between the various Unixen when it comes
to configuration. 

>    This last issue drives up cost
>    as the manpower needed to admin all those different UNIX becomes
>    significant over any one-vendor solution, whethe a UNIX based
>    solution or something else.

Granted - it's easier to admin a HP-UX only site, but adding
another Unix to the mix isn't nearly as difficult as adding, say,
Windows. All Unixen understand NIS, NFS, ssh, etc. At the office,
I have HP-UX, AIX, Solaris, and Linux. On all these, I get the
same home directory, use the same commands, and build software
from the same makefiles. The basic infrastructure is essentially
unchanged since 1991 (when we started). There are more systems,
and its more diverse, but fundamentally, the network concepts
are still the same. 
With Windows, I've migrated from no network (3.0), to beta-testing
the MS TCP/IP stack (3.1) with Workgroup style networking, NT 4.0 
PDC/BDC/WINS style domains (thank God for Samba, which saved me from
having to dedicate PCs to being PDCs or BDCs), and now Win2K Advanced
Directory - but I'll need to change that when we'll go on to XP,
because AD version 2 is not compatible with AD version 1. 
And let me tell you that each and everyone of the Windows versions 
I've used has a different set of screens to configure the network.

>    And I still insist that an open system is one I can see inside.  I
>    can affort a source listing for VMS.  I can generally get Linux
>    source off the net, but I still don't know anyone who can afford
>    a Solaris, HP-UX, AIX, ... proprietary UNIX license.

I paid $75 for a Solaris 8 Foundation license; I ordered it on-line
from Sun. 

Obviously, there are vendor-only extensions to the Unix systems
from the different vendors, but the fact remains that Unix is the
only OS that you can buy from several vendors, and the only OS
that'll allow you to change vendors in case of problems. You can't
do that with Windows, or VMS, or RTE.

