In article <abdjh3$kog$1@news1.kornet.net>, "Atom" <aiguru@linux.co.kr> 
wrote:

X First, sorry for my poor English..
X 
X set ts=4
X set cindent
X 
X How do I save vi configuration above..

As another reply has already stated, you create a file $HOME/.exrc and 
for security reasons you should own it, and it should only allow you to 
write it, and hopefully you $HOME directory only allows you to create 
files in it.

You can also put a limited number of vi settings in the EXINIT 
environment variable:

        setenv EXINIT "set ts=4 cindent"

however, if you have an EXINIT, then $HOME/.exrc is not read, unless you 
also include a source $HOME/.exrc in your EXINIT environment variable:

        setenv EXINIT "source $HOME/.exrc | set ts=4 cindent"

However, there is _NO_ cindent option in the Tru64 UNIX version of vi, 
so this has me wondering if perhaps you are running a vi emulation such 
as the execllent Vim <www.vim.org> or <vim.sourceforge.net>?

If you are running Vim, then you would put your Vim initialization 
settings in a $HOME/.vimrc file, or use VIMINIT environment variable.

And if you are using Vim I can recomment the book:

        Vi IMproved - Vim
        by Steve Oualline
        New Riders Publishing
        ISBN: 0735710015

Good luck.

                                        Bob Harris


