> > Did you check this? The configuration files are under /etc/lp,
> > and they're all text files. 
> 
> Only if the printers are connected to the LPT port.  If you have
> network LPD or LPR printers,  then the ASCII text files tell you
> very little.  For instance I have a printer attached to my NT
> PC that I access via LPR.  What files actually point to its location:
> 
>    $ find /var/spool/lp \
>    >      /etc/lp \
>    >      /usr/lib/lp 
>    >      -follow \
>    >      -type f \
>    >      -exec grep -iq w09876 {} \; \
>    >      -exec file {} \;
>    /var/spool/lp/request/w09876/.sendingstatus:    ascii text
>    /var/spool/lp/pstatus:  data
>    /var/spool/lp/qstatus:  data
>    /var/spool/lp/default:  ascii text
>    /var/spool/lp/outputq:  data
> 
> The three files that control where the output is sent,  pstatus
> qstatus amd outputq are all binaries.  Their contents are
> constructed by the lpadmin command and only queryable by the lpstat
> command.  I have never been able to figure out how to clone a
> configuration from one LPD system to another.

I've no intentions to start splitting hairs. LPD has been 
implemented anew for almost every strain of SVID compliant
Unix. The current big three (Solaris, AIX and HP-UX) each
have a different implementation. For example, lpadmin is
a binary in HP-UX, an shell script in Solaris, and absent
in AIX...

Nonetheless, files in /var (even when they are created
through a command that has "admin" as part of its name)
shouldn't really be configuration files, but rather
run-time files. The HP-UX implementation of LPD is rather
crummy, because the only place it stores the destination
is in pstatus, making it effectively a configuration file.
Naugthy, naughty, naughty HP. Solaris is better, and stores
this information in /etc/printers.conf.

Thanks for letting me remember one of the myriad of reasons
why LPD sucks rocks through a straw.


