What I did(a few years ago and this is from memory) was to create a 640Mb
partition on a hard disk, dd the raw image of the tru64 boot cd onto it
and start from there. I made as many modifications as I could get away
with and eventually came up with something that would boot as I wanted and
start a custom restore.
Then I dd'd that back onto a disk image and used cdrecord to burn it out.
The important bits I learned were that I couldn't make an alpha boot a
cdfs disk, that I could make a ufs filesystem on a CD, and that I couldn't
get disklabel to write bootblocks into a file.
I haven't tried any of this for a long time though (I was using 3.2G and planning
for the upgrade to Version 4 at the time when I made this work).
Hope this helps some
Adam




