>    Some Linux use BSD-style text configuration files that can be edited
>    by hand.  Other's seem to use SVID-style binary configuration files
>    that can't readily be edited by hand.

Never saw that. Could you tell us an example where is no text source
available?

>    And nothing in the typical Linux distribution points out which tools
>    are in common with other vendor's Linux, so the kid has no way to
>    know which tools to choose.

Kids should not administrate boxes but if they're good hackers.

>    How minor the differences are depend on what work you're doing.  At
>    the end user level all UNIX are pretty much the same.

So a text terminal is pretty much the same as a GNOME-Desktop?
And CDE is pretty much the same as WindowMaker?

For the end user, *NIX has that UI the administrator chose (somtimes it
has an ugly UI the vendor offers as default if there was an incompetent
system engineer).

>    At the
>    programmer level real differences start to show up.  To the admin
>    every UNIX is dramaticaly different.  This last issue drives up cost
>    as the manpower needed to admin all those different UNIX becomes
>    significant over any one-vendor solution, whethe a UNIX based
>    solution or something else.

I'm sorry, but this is incredible nonsense. Today I manage SuSE, RedHat,
Debian, sometimes Slackware Boxes, I have NetBSD, FreeBSD, MacOS-X here,
I do in Solaris, HP-UX and SCO Unix, sometimes other derivates of Unix.
You just need to know the difference between a BSD init and a System V
init and grep -r. It helps if you know the rc.config and rc.config.d
concept many System V or System V like Unices have. And it helps to read
and grep the scripts of course.

It is necessary to understand the basic terms, which are the same at
every *NIX Box, that is true.

An "admin" who is only able to "point and click" should stop his/her
work _immediately_ and learn. Fortunately there is no "Minesweeper
Certified Solitaire Engineer" for Unix or something like that. And the
"titles" like "Slowlaris-Expert" or "HPsUX Expert" are bad enough.

>    And I still insist that an open system is one I can see inside.  I
>    can affort a source listing for VMS.  I can generally get Linux
>    source off the net, but I still don't know anyone who can afford
>    a Solaris, HP-UX, AIX, ... proprietary UNIX license.

*NIX mainly is a "text-processing application". So you have enough
source code for general purposes anyway, you just don't need to know
kernel source or source of binary tools for administrational purposes.
Just read the man pages and have a look at the scripts. Especially do
read the boot scripts when you have your first contact with a new *NIX.

Hint: try to bring up a Linux box from scratch. You will learn many
useful things, even much about Unices themselves.

VB.
