> >>    that can't readily be edited by hand.

> > Doesn't really matter. What those tools end up doing are much the same
> > things in either case... in fact a great way to learn would be to go in
> > to the startup scripts and trace everything through and see where the
> > configuration information gets converted to actual changes in system state.

>    What makes you think all configuration files are written by startup
>    scripts?

How does "see where the configuration information gets converted to actual
changes in system state" translate to anything even vaguely resembling "all
configuration files are written by startup scripts"?

Regardless of how the configuration is managed by the configuration tools, it
has to be read from that database and converted into changes in the text files
that the UNIX daemons that actually do the work use, and to changes in internal
state that can be read and later written by command line programs.

Therefore, regardless of what the tools do, they can always be replaced by the
files they eventually generate and update, and by a sequence of commands run by
the startup scripts. Determining what those files and commands are is a perfect
way to learn how UNIX really works.

One caveat: there are two exceptions to this general rule that I know of:
hosted implementations of UNIX like Microsoft's Interix and IBM's AIX will
require you to learn the underlying operating system's tools, rather than
the UNIX ones, for system management. And an extensive enough shell, like
the MacOS X layers above Darwin, may have its own approach to configuration
management that isn't always visible from standard UNIX tools.

But for any conventional UNIX system, including any Linux distribution I've
ever looked at (and I've looked at a fair few), the front ends can always be
bypassed and replaced. Even for SCO, which has more than its fair share of
creeping horrors.

