X Which *kernel* is the new "HP-UX" using?

HP-UX kernel.

Please keep in mind that before TruClusters, Tru64 UNIX (aka OSF/1, aka 
Digital UNIX) did not have cluster support.  It had to be added on.  The 
only reference model was OpenVMS and since most of that code was in 
Bliss, only concepts could be moved to Tru64 UNIX.

Well, now at least there is a working code base written in C that can be 
ported.  Changes, as necessary, to the HP-UX kernel will be made, just 
like they were to Tru64 UNIX.  But there is that working reference model 
already in place.  Many utlities will not need any changes since the 
APIs will be implemented as is.  There is real world customer 
experiences available to help in design decision trade-offs.  And most 
important of all.  Many of the people that implemented TruClusters on 
Tru64 UNIX are going to be doing the work.  That is a huge advantage.

It will be a lot of hard work, and most likely some late nights and 
weekends getting it to work, but that is nothing new to the software 
development community.

----

And another comment about what will and will not be ported to HP-UX.  
For the most part engineers at HP and Compaq have not been allowed to 
talk to each other.  Legally we were still competing to win customers.  
So now that the companies are legally merged, it is the first time many 
of the lower level managers and technical lead engineers can compare 
notes and lobby for the best features of Tru64 UNIX to be considered for 
porting.  That will take time to make those evaluations (considering 
each will need to continue to support the existing customer bases).

