Journaling is great.  But (as the other poster said) it's not a replacement
for a proper fsck.  When you try to rm a file and get an error like 'no such
inode' or something, you know your filesystem has bit rot, and you want a tool
that will root through your filesystem with neurotic perfection, pointing out
and (if possible) correcting every conceivable flaw.

Fsck is not a luxury--it's an absolute requirement.

> > One of them won't even support using a vt100 as its console.  It's like
> > they're moving backwards in time.
> 
> So you think vt100 is the future ?

VT100s (or the alternative hardware terminal of your choice) are really stupid
and they work really really well.  When I press a key, I know exactly what the
OS is going to see.  They never break.  When they do break, you can swap a new
one in in five seconds.

If my Unix vendor tells me they don't support hardware terminals and that
their vision of the future is that I have to use a (very expensive) PC running
a flaky app on top of a flaky OS to talk to my Unix console, I'm not going to
be a happy camper.

Mike


