Linux is unix-like, certainly, but there are differences here and
there owing to its independent ancestry. For example, in unix one
has raw and cooked devices, under different names, so that one might
access a disk partition as /dev/hda3 to do normal file operations,
or as /dev/rhda3 to access it as a nonfile structured device. In Linux
the same name does for both and the distinction comes with use,
not with name. 

Linux is not descended from ATT code, is as entitled to say it is not
unix as Gnu (Gnu's Not Unix) is.

The defining of a standard API with remarks like "anything that satisfies
this API is Unix" has been interesting to watch. However, it has come
very close (and may reach the point) to allowing one to say VMS is Unix,
which those familiar with the systems would find a tad odd.

Linux is of course very very close to unix when by "unix" I mean "systems
descended from the ATT code original". (Granted the BSDs have replaced
all the ATT code, but the fact of descent cannot be denied historically.)

It would not be too surprising if the evolution of unix and linux (and Hurd
and some others) leads to a single overall system though.



