Bob Koehler wrote:
> 
>    Even my kid's Linux is available only from one vendor, runs on
>    only certain hardware, 

All operating systems run only on limited hardware, that is the
nature of things that control hardware. Linux runs on more than
most.

>    and can be managed only via that vendor's
>    choice of admin tools.  If he had to change to another Linux he'd
>    have to learn his admin stuff all over again.

Only if he choose to use non-free vendor supplied tools and not
the many excellent free software based tools bundled, you can
always choose to use proprietary software, but as an admin who
crosses Unix flavours it is more useful to learn more general
tools whether it be webmin, useradd, or vi.

Face it, HP's SAM was for a long time the only GUI based Unix
admin tool worth using, (SMIT wasn't bad), but the other vendors
bundled tools were pretty much useless. SAM's main advantage was
the extensive checks it would do before letting you reallocate
disk space - saved my data from typos a couple of times -
otherwise most admins just scripted the things they needed to
do.

I think SuSE is the only major Linux vendor supplying extensive
collections of non-free admin tools to the consumer market, so
within flavours of Linux you are unlikely to have to relearn
substantial amounts, just install your favourite tools and you
are away.

I find SuSE admin tools just get in the way so whilst it is a
great Desktop OS, I won't be using it for server side stuff if I
get to specify the flavour.



