Linus controls the "standard" kernels pretty tightly, but I can't see how he
can prevent people from shipping modified kernels without keeping them from
adding their own bugfixes. If he was doing that, then you'd see lots of
little "patchkit" fixes being distributed along, the way you did with 386BSD
when Jolitz was in charge, or the way you get with qmail.

It can't be that Linus and his minions are so fast at integrating changes
that nobody ever needs to do this. First, it would be insanely difficult
for them to integrate every possible kernel tweak that anyone might need
into the kernel... second, the behaviour of Linux users when a new release
is coming up suggests otherwise. :->

