Mike Coleman wrote:
> 
> I'd been away from propietary Unix for a couple years, using only Linux, and I
> recently have started interacting with a couple of flavors of proprietary
> Unix.  What's amazed me is how *immature* these Unices seem compared to
> Linux.

Yes but it is maturity in different areas. Linux has more rough
edges worn off through more users. The GNU file utilities are
superb, far surpassing the originals in ease of use,
functionality, and correctness(!) (Okay I've only hit one bug in
HP-UX cp command, but that is one bug more than I've hit on GNU
cp - bugs in cp hurt!)

The issue with Enterprise systems is a moving target, many of
the "enterprise" level systems I have worked with have been
surpassed during their life times by the CPU power of the
desktop workstations they were supplying services to.

So we (well those who date back even a little way) tend to think
of Enterprise computing as about big iron, when in fact it is,
and has been for quite a while, about redundancy, security and
availability, and the related issues of managability. Raw power
is only an issue in as much as the vendors have bloated their
offerings to eat the resources available, even then most of the
big applications will run happily on a server powered with an
off the shelf state of the art INTEL clone, till you scale to
quite large enterprises.

The march to network storage raises other issues as well. The
big Unix servers (and IBM Mainframes) power came in large part
from expandable I/O, and appropriate bus technology, but where
the storage is provided over Gigabit ethernet, or Fibre Channel,
and the buses are largely commodity products, that advantage is
lost.

With some of the storage technologies the "OS" is really an
irritation required to bootstrap an Oracle database or supply an
authentication service, or fire up a GUI, that is why basic file
serving is already moving to the storage network hardware. Once
you move the storage out of a modern UNIX system you lose much
of the value add. LVM is nice, but most implementations don't
compete well with the network storage devices (Even if they are
from the same software company underneath).

The Enterprise Unix (and mini) systems won't die suddenly, but
the number of people with needs not met by Linux will decrease
steadily, driving it into a minority interest. SUN, and to a
lesser extent HP, seem to have recognised this, and gone for the
low end server market aggressively, but do you really want to be
competing on price with Intel clone servers?


