will reject responsibility for the "porting" of Windows to large
server systems to the hardware manufacturer, and because they are
severly limited in the modifications they can make to Windows, the
performance and stability of the system will be impacted. When it
comes to Linux, the problem is similar, but caused by the fact that
the Linux kernel hackers don't have access to the larger (server
class) systems. 

> because typically speaking the user doesn't
> want to deal with the OS, he/she/it only cares about running the app
> quickly and securely.

That obviously surmises that the particular application runs
on the OS that comes with the hardware you selected. 

> I can administrate anything, given time and
> education, so the platform that I use is completely and utterly
> irrelevant in my equations - how fast the hardware works is all I care
> about.  So far nothing but the proprietaries have pulled it off.

Certainly at the server (large system) level, the OSes designed for
commodity hardware falter because of the lack of "exposure" to the
high-end hardware. It's easier to downsize an OS that runs a
128-way multiprocessor to handle a workstation, than to upsize an
OS that runs almost exclusively on a uniprocessor to handle a
128-way behemoth.

> Show me a Linux/Win2K build and architecture that can defeat my C3700
> workstations, my N4000 550 8 way on a 150,000 element structural model
> and I will gladly convert over.
> 
> Until then, I'll stick to my proprietary guns.

