This is my guess, maybe someone else has their ideas as well.
Assuming you have a 1gb RAM system

1 gb for Quorum
2gb for the Initial Tru64 Install ( depending on amount of ram you have) /,
/usr, /var & swap
3 gb for member1 boot (/boot partition = 1gb  & local machine swap = 2gb)
3 gb for member2 boot (/boot partition = 1gb  & local machine swap = 2gb)
8 gb for cluster /, cluster /usr & cluster /var (these are clustered file
systems for all machines)
   Breakdown maybe cluster /         = 2 gb
                                 cluster /usr     = 4 gb
                                 cluster /var     = 2 gb

Since you may use 3 * 36 gb disks in Raid 5 you will probably have around
18-19gb left.  I assume you will want to use that.  So create a partition
with the remainder of the Raid5 array. You can use that disk as a /data
volume with advfs.

Note:
If you want more storage, once the cluster is up and running there is no
need for the initial Tru64 install device.  You can blow this away and use
it as a cluster filesystem However that device is very handy if the cluster
goes belly up (ie rm -rf * from /)

You will still have 4 slots or so on the storage system to create other
devices whatever they may be.  Two raid 5 sets are better than 1 big one.
You should use 1 Raid 5 for Cluster and 1 for Data.

Another Option is to not use swap on the shared storage but use swap on the
local disk in each machine (If you have them), this will then decrease the
size of the memberX boot partitions.

Any questions change the "test-1234" in my mail to "tpomeroy"

Regards

Tony



