"Dave Gudewicz" <david.gudewicz@abbott.com> wrote in message
news:aaudu1$58b$1@fizban.fizban.pprd.abbott.com...
> I guess I need some help.  Could someone please explain how LINUX is
really
> NOT UNIX?
>
> I don't mean beneath the cloak of branding, etc.  I mean under the hood.

Well, the most obvious reason is that it's really not ready for
enterprise-level use, just as Windows isn't (though Linux and even Windows
might some day be):  it doesn't have reasonable SMP capabilities for
anything over 4 to at most 8 processors, it doesn't have any
prime-time-ready clustering mechanisms, it's still experimenting
(vigorously) with file systems, it has no formal support structure (though
some of the package vendors are trying to create them)...

From what I know of Linux I like it a lot.  But it's not a mature platform
yet for much beyond desktop (or at least development desktop) and low-end
server use.

Now, that may not be a real answer to the question you asked, but it does
illustrate that, yet again, Curly is trashing his own products without any
effective replacement up his sleeve.  In many senses, of course Linux *is*
Unix - which means that rather than having to convert to Linux, many
customers will likely choose to remain with their current Unix vendor as
long as that vendor makes sure that their Unix is Linux-compatible (which
even VMS could be).  That certainly seems to be IBM's approach to the issue,
and by all appearances its customers like it:  it gives them both the
advantages of a mature platform and the advantages of a standard environment
without vendor lock-in.

- bill





