In article <ud5cb99fqvtq85@news.supernews.com>,
John Vottero <John@mvpsi.com> wrote:
> I have to agree with Capellas, in fact, it seems obvious to me.  You can no
> longer sell Unix by claiming that it's the "Open/Standard" O/S.

Funny, I have no problem running the same software on Tru64 and FreeBSD
as the next guy over runs on Solaris and Linux and the bloke down the
street runs on AIX and HPUX.

> Linux/NetBSD/FreeBSD are the only true open/standard operating systems.

Don't confuse Open Source with Open Systems. Open Systems are a matter
of open interfaces and protocols, documentation and standards that allow
independant implementations to work together.

> Unix vendors have to start pointing at their proprietary extensions as the
> reason to buy their flavor of *nix.

Well, yes. Just as Ford and Chevy point at their features and strengths as
reasons to buy their cars... but they still all burn the same gas and
travel the same highways. I don't have to wait for someone to build a new
bridge over the Atchafalaya to go from Houston to New Orleans just because
I'm driving a Ford instead of a Chevy.

Similarly, if I'm running Tru64 and I need to talk to a Solaris box, I don't
need to worry whether Sun has unilaterally modified NFS to keep my box from
seeing its file systems, and if I've written an application for one I can
run it on the other.

These open interfaces and protocols are so compelling that Microsoft is
integrating Interix into Windows Services for UNIX 3.0 ... which basically
turns Windows NT into UNIX as far as an applications program is concerned.

It even uses the "evil" GNU-licensed GCC.

Just like MacOS X does.

Looks like any system you buy in the future is going to be UNIX, one way or
the other.

