"Bill Todd" <billtodd@metrocast.net> writes:
> [Linux is] still experimenting (vigorously) with file systems,

There are experimental filesystems available, and vigorous progress is being
made on new ones, but the basic ext2 filesystem that everyone uses is rock
solid.  And it comes with a working fsck program.  Can your proprietary Unix
say the same?  :-/

> But it's not a mature platform yet for much beyond desktop (or at least
> development desktop) and low-end server use.

I'd been away from propietary Unix for a couple years, using only Linux, and I
recently have started interacting with a couple of flavors of proprietary
Unix.  What's amazed me is how *immature* these Unices seem compared to
Linux.  One of them won't even support using a vt100 as its console.  It's
like they're moving backwards in time.

I think Capellas is correct that the mid-term future is free Unix and
Windows.  I'm not so sure that growth in Linux is all coming from proprietary
Unix, though.  If there were no free Unix, Windows would probably have even a
larger share, so in that sense, Linux growth *is* coming out of Windows hide.
And if the markets are allowed to work, I believe this will continue.

Mike


