Beowulf clusters are designed for a considerably different problem space than
VMS clusters.  Beowulf clusters are designed, last I looked, to make a virtual
supercomputer out of a bunch of cheap PC boxes.  A Beowulf cluster will
typically be running one massive computation at a time; it's not really
designed to enable commercial application processing to continue relatively
unimpeded while one out of three of your data centers burns down, nor does it
do the job of allowing multiple processes safe access to resources mediated by
a distributed lock manager.

Beowulf is fine, but it's not even trying to address the space Bill is talking
about.


