Michael Kraemer wrote:

> >
> > There are experimental filesystems available, and vigorous progress is being
> > made on new ones, but the basic ext2 filesystem that everyone uses is rock
> > solid.  And it comes with a working fsck program.  Can your proprietary Unix
> > say the same?  :-/
>
> As an AIX user and sysadmin I haven't used fsck in the past 10 or 12
> years. Can you say JFS or LVM ?
>

LVM is not part of the problem.
JFS has an fsck, it's just that you don't normally ever see it.
If you don't shutdown the filesystem properly, it's left in an inconsistant
state. When the system comes backup then it fsck's the thing for you.

What JFS gives is good reliable data to feed into fsck. With a journaled
filesystem fsck knows how to recover from a impropper shutdown. A
non journaled filesystem doesn't so it just guesses.

In most cases non journaled filesystems fsck also fix the filesystem OK
they just take MUCH longer to do it.

I don't know the IBM JFS that well, but with the Veritas one then I know
that it also has a long hand look at everything guess how to fix it mode.
The journal only helps in the case of an impropper shutdown. If the filesystem
actually gets corrupt at some other time, then it still needs to fsck the old way.

Cheers

Ken




