"Volker Birk" <bumens@dingens.org> wrote in message
news:3cd3cca4$0$228$4d4ebb8e@businessnews.de.uu.net...
> In comp.sys.hp.hpux "Alan Winston - SSRL Admin Cmptg Mgr"
<winston@ssrl.slac.stanford.edu> wrote:
> > Beowulf clusters are designed for a considerably different problem space
than
> > VMS clusters.
>
> That is partly right - and completely off-topic.

Wrong.  The subject under discussion was the lack of prime-time-level
maturity of Linux clustering.  VMS *defines* clustering maturity; Linux is
still trying to understand that definition.

 VMS has nothing to do
> with Unix.

Wrong.  VMS was, in fact, one of the first POSIX-compliant platforms.  While
POSIX != Unix, it's a bit of a stretch to claim 'POSIX has nothing to do
with Unix'.

>
> A commercial fail-over solution is offered by HP for example, or have a
look
> at http://www.missioncriticallinux.com/.

Fail-over-style clustering is an improvement (though scripted fail-over
chains remain a kludge), but still falls short in areas like
concurrent-shared-disk access and distributed lock management (plus a whole
bunch of ancillary non-disk-related areas).  Not to mention that last I
heard missioncriticallinux was not in the best of health (which is too bad,
since it seemed to be the most credible of the Linux efforts in the
prime-time space even if not yet mature).

There's a good reason so much cluster-related development activity is still
taking place (at a still-accelerating rate) in the Linux community:  it's
because mature facilities simply aren't there yet.

>
> You want to do that just using free software? We'll build a solution for
> you (we're using ICP Vortex RAID cluster & scripting).

Hey, *I* can build a solution for you too.  But I wouldn't call it 'mature'
until it had been out in the field for a few years.

>
> You want a real virtual machine redundant cluster build with free
software?
> No problem, have a look at http://www.linuxvirtualserver.org/.

Fine for read-only access with a central dispatcher, but that's another very
limiting niche.  I think you need to read (or possibly re-read) Greg
Pfister's "In Search of Clusters" (2nd edition) to understand what
'maturity' means in this space.

>
> > If you're using VMS already, why not keep using VMS until and unless
forced
> > to move?
>
> Yes, why not? I'm not defending Linux here. I only want to show, that
> the "Linux is Unix for children" theses is not true anymore.

True:  Linux is now Unix for adolescents.  Some proprietary Unixes are for
adults, though youngish ones.  Systems like VMS and zOS qualify as 'mature'
(it's possible something like Netware might as well).

- bill





